Let $d\ge1$ and let $\Omega\subset\mathbb R^d$ be a nonempty bounded connected open polyhedral set, meaning the interior of a finite union of bounded closed convex polytopes. A closed convex polytope is a bounded intersection of finitely many closed half-spaces with nonempty interior. A **cell-face mesh** $\mathcal T=(\mathcal K,\mathcal F)$ consists of finitely many nonempty cells $K=\operatorname{int}P_K\subset\Omega$, where each $P_K$ is a closed convex polytope, with pairwise disjoint interiors and $\overline\Omega=\bigcup_{K\in\mathcal K}\overline K$, together with the relative interiors of the $(d-1)$-dimensional faces of their closures. Each interior face $F\in\mathcal F_{\rm int}$ is incident to exactly two cells $K,L$ and is denoted $F=K|L$; each boundary face $F\in\mathcal F_{\rm bdy}$ is incident to one cell and is contained in $\partial\Omega$. In dimension one a face is an endpoint and has measure $|F|=1$.

Write $|K|$ for the $d$-dimensional volume of $K$, $|F|$ for the $(d-1)$-dimensional measure of $F$, $n_{K,F}$ for the unit normal outward from $K$, $x_K$ for a chosen point in $K$, and $x_F$ for the centroid of $F$. For every incident pair set $d_{K,F}=\operatorname{dist}(x_K,F)>0$. The **cell adjacency graph** has one vertex for each cell and an edge for each interior face. A mesh is **boundary anchored** if every cell has a finite path in this graph to a cell incident to a boundary face; such a path can be chosen simple, with no repeated cells.

For a family $(\mathcal T_h)$, put $h=\max_{K\in\mathcal K}\operatorname{diam}K$. The family is **uniformly regular** if there are fixed constants $c,C>0$ and $N_0\in\mathbb N$ such that, for every mesh and each of its cells and faces,
$$ c h^d\le |K|\le C h^d,\qquad c h^{d-1}\le |F|\le C h^{d-1},\qquad c h\le d_{K,F}\le C h, $$
and each cell has at most $N_0$ faces. A family is **refining** if $h\to0$. These bounds are invoked only where explicitly assumed; a cell-face mesh alone is not asserted to be uniformly regular or orthogonal.

The finite-volume convention is that a face flux $Q_{K,F}$ is signed outward from $K$. If $F=K|L$ is an interior face, local conservation requires the single shared-flux identity $Q_{K,F}+Q_{L,F}=0$. The mesh drawing below labels the center-to-center vector and the outward normal; no orthogonality is part of this general mesh definition.

```tikz
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1]
  \draw[thick] (0,0) -- (2,0) -- (2,2) -- (0,2) -- cycle;
  \draw[thick] (2,0) -- (4.3,0.5) -- (4.3,2.5) -- (2,2) -- cycle;
  \draw[thick] (2,0) -- (2,2);
  \node at (1,1) {$K$};
  \node at (3.15,1.25) {$L$};
  \node at (2,1.45) {$F=K|L$};
  \fill (1,1) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$x_K$};
  \fill (3.15,1.25) circle (2pt) node[below right] {$x_L$};
  \draw[->,blue,thick] (1,1) -- (3.15,1.25) node[midway,below] {$x_L-x_K$};
  \draw[->,red,thick] (2,1) -- (2.8,1) node[right] {$n_{K,F}$};
  \draw[->,red,thick] (2,1.8) -- (1.2,1.8) node[left] {$n_{L,F}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
```

The cell-face mesh definition and the diagram fix only the objects, incidences, normals, and orientation. A two-point flux approximation adds a separate orthogonality condition; the hybrid mimetic method is formulated on the general cell-face geometry specified here.

Let $\mathcal T=(\mathcal K,\mathcal F)$ be a boundary-anchored mesh as in [[def-fv-admissible-cell-face-mesh]]. It is **TPFA-admissible** if, for each interior face $F=K|L$, the center segment $[x_K,x_L]$ is perpendicular to the supporting hyperplane of $F$ and intersects the relative interior of $F$ at a point $y_F$. Thus the center points lie on opposite sides of that face, and $d_{K,F}=|y_F-x_K|$, $d_{L,F}=|x_L-y_F|$, so define $d_F:=|x_L-x_K|=d_{K,F}+d_{L,F}$ for every interior face. Each boundary face $F\subset\partial K\cap\partial\Omega$ must satisfy $x_F-x_K=d_{K,F}n_{K,F}$ with $d_{K,F}>0$; in particular, its face centroid is the normal projection of $x_K$ onto the face plane.

Let the scalar diffusion coefficient be constant on each cell, $\lambda|_K=\lambda_K$, with fixed bounds $0<\lambda_*\le\lambda_K\le\lambda^*<\infty$. For each interior face and boundary face define the transmissibilities
$$ T_F=\frac{|F|}{d_{K,F}/\lambda_K+d_{L,F}/\lambda_L}\quad(F=K|L),\qquad T_{K,F}=\frac{|F|\lambda_K}{d_{K,F}}\quad(F\subset\partial K\cap\partial\Omega). $$
The interior formula is the conductance of two half-cell resistances in series. Equivalently, with $\lambda_F^{\rm harm}=d_F/(d_{K,F}/\lambda_K+d_{L,F}/\lambda_L)$, it is $T_F=|F|\lambda_F^{\rm harm}/d_F$. This is a distance-weighted harmonic mean; it equals the ordinary harmonic mean only when $d_{K,F}=d_{L,F}$.

For cell values $u_K$ and prescribed boundary face values $g_F$, the outward signed flux is
$$ Q_{K,F}(u)=T_F(u_K-u_L)\quad(F=K|L),\qquad Q_{K,F}(u)=T_{K,F}(u_K-g_F)\quad(F\subset\partial\Omega). $$
This is a **two-point flux approximation (TPFA)**: an interior face flux uses only the values in its two incident cells, and a boundary face flux uses only its incident cell value and the prescribed boundary value.
The finite-volume equations for a cellwise integrable source $f$ are
$$ \sum_{F\subset\partial K}Q_{K,F}(u)=\int_K f(x)\,dx\quad(K\in\mathcal K). $$
Thus an interior face uses one flux with opposite signs in its two balances. For homogeneous Dirichlet data define the symmetric energy form and its norm by
$$ a_h(u,v)=\sum_{F=K|L\in\mathcal F_{\rm int}}T_F(u_K-u_L)(v_K-v_L)+\sum_{F\in\mathcal F_{\rm bdy}}T_{K,F}u_Kv_K,\qquad \|u\|_{E_h}^2=a_h(u,u). $$
For each face take a separate copy $D_F$ of measure $|F|d_F$ on an interior face and $|F|d_{K,F}$ on a boundary face. The reconstructed normal gradient is $(u_L-u_K)n_{K,F}/d_F$ on an interior face (with either fixed orientation) and $-u_Kn_{K,F}/d_{K,F}$ on a homogeneous boundary face. Give the interior face copy coefficient $\lambda_F^{\rm harm}$ and the boundary face copy coefficient $\lambda_K$. Then $\|u\|_{E_h}^2$ is exactly the weighted $L^2$ norm of this face-copy gradient reconstruction. The copies are bookkeeping spaces; they are not claimed to partition $\Omega$.

